  "id": "Compound::DB01154 - Gene::2566",
  "verbalization": "Thiamylal, a thiamine antagonist, is shown to bind to Gabra6, a subunit of the GABA-A receptor, which is involved in GABA-a receptor activity. This activity is then linked to Gabrg2, a subunit of the GABA-A receptor, suggesting a potential role in the receptor's function. The evidence from the three paths indicates that Thiamylal may interact with Gabra6, which in turn participates in GABA-a receptor activity, ultimately leading to Gabrg2's involvement. The consistent binding of Thiamylal to Gabra6 across all three paths, along with the shared participation in GABA-a receptor activity, supports a mechanism where Thiamylal influences the GABA-A receptor complex through Gabra6. The presence of Gabrg2 as a participant in this process suggests that Thiamylal may modulate GABA-A receptor function, potentially through interactions with Gabra6. The integration of these pathways highlights a possible mechanism by which Thiamylal affects GABA-A receptor activity, with Gabrg2 being a key target in this process.",
